 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "Telluride 45 in (115 cm): Freezing Level Rises to 4,280 m",
   "description": "Telluride holds 115 cm (45 in) on 8 November. The freezing level climbs to 4,280 m (14,042 ft) on 9 November, bringing rain-on-snow risk to the highest slopes.",
   "lede": "Forecasts show the freezing level will climb above Telluride's summit on 9 November, reaching 4,280 m (14,042 ft). When rain falls onto existing snow at high elevation and then refreezes, it creates an icy crust that bonds the layers—a significant change in thin early-season snowpack.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "When Rain Reaches the Ridge",
     "p": [
      "The freezing level—the altitude where air temperature reaches 0 °C (32 °F)—is projected to surge on 9 November, peaking at 4,280 m (14,042 ft) according to our forecast model. This elevation exceeds Telluride's summit lift at 3,812 m (12,507 ft). When rain falls onto snow at high elevations and then refreezes in colder hours, it creates an icy crust that bonds the underlying layers and stiffens the surface.",
      "In November, when total snow depth remains shallow across the region, such consolidation alters the skiing texture more dramatically than it would later in winter. The event itself is brief—the freeze line drops sharply after 9 November—but the structural change persists."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Ranking the Depths",
     "p": [
      "Kendall Mountain leads the five resorts modeled, with 120 cm (47 in) on the upper slopes as of 8 November, followed closely by Telluride at 115 cm (45 in). Wolf Creek and Eagle Point hold around 88–87 cm (35–34 in), while Lee's Ski Hill measures 78 cm (31 in). These reflect estimates for the highest terrain of each mountain.",
      "Snow concentrates sharply at elevation. Telluride's mid-slope shows only 14 cm (5.5 in) according to the model, illustrating how rapidly depth diminishes below the peaks. At base elevations, some areas show zero depth, confirming that early-season snow remains patchy and elevation-dependent."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "What the Instruments Recorded",
     "p": [
      "Ground stations provide the most reliable evidence. On 1 November, Rat Creek at 3,560 m (11,676 ft) reported 66 cm (26 in), with 23 cm (9 in) arriving in just 24 hours. Beartown, also near 3,560 m (11,676 ft), matched that depth. Red Mountain Pass at 3,371 m (11,059 ft) held 61 cm (24 in) after accumulating 28 cm (11 in) in a single day.",
      "These measurements demonstrate that high-elevation storms genuinely filled the summits, not merely glazed them with wind-scoured surface snow. Station data lags our model by a week but anchors confidence: when instruments show 60+ cm, we know substantial snow sits in the high country."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Three Days Ahead",
     "p": [
      "Our model contains no precipitation between 9 and 11 November across this region. The freeze line is forecast to descend steeply on 10–11 November, reaching as low as 2,680 m (8,793 ft) on 11 November. This rapid modulation—warm and wet on the 9th, then cold on the 11th—creates the classic freeze-thaw cycle that consolidates snow.",
      "The regional picture shows contraction: snow-covered area drops from 186,330 km² today to 147,690 km² by 11 November, a 21% loss in three days. This shrinkage reflects both rain-on-snow melt at higher elevations and the broader warming trend ahead."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "The Bigger Picture",
     "p": [
      "Across the region, 146 of 195 resorts hold some snow on 8 November, down from 151 resorts on 6 November. The model projects only 132 resorts with cover by 11 November. This fluctuation is routine in early season, when each degree of warming reshapes conditions.",
      "What matters is that high elevations retain meaningful depth despite the thaw. Telluride, Wolf Creek, and Kendall Mountain remain the anchors holding the deepest material through the transition."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "What to Watch",
     "p": [
      "Skiers considering the highest terrain at Telluride or Wolf Creek should expect icy and consolidated conditions after 10 November, as rain from the 9th refreezes overnight. Early-season navigation is technical: depth looks reasonable at the summits but drops rapidly below, and rocks remain common.",
      "Consult the Colorado Avalanche Information Center bulletin before venturing off-piste; current danger levels are rated 1 on the 1–5 scale. No new snow is forecast in the coming days, so conditions will harden rather than soften. Thin, stable snow at high elevation deserves respect."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "Watch for icy conditions on north-facing terrain above 3,800 m (12,500 ft) after the rain-refreeze cycle on 9–10 November.",
    "Navigate terrain carefully below the summit: depth drops sharply from 115 cm at the top to 14 cm at mid-elevation on Telluride. Rocks remain exposed in thin-cover zones.",
    "Ground stations measured 60+ cm at elevations above 3,350 m (11,000 ft) on 1 November; use this as reference for where continuous cover begins.",
    "The freezing level stays below 2,700 m (8,900 ft) on 11 November, so lower elevations will soften slightly but not consolidate as severely as the summits.",
    "Check the Colorado Avalanche Information Center bulletin daily; stability is rated low (1 on 1–5 scale) but rain-on-snow events can alter conditions rapidly."
   ],
   "captions": {
    "map": "Forecast freezing level on 9 November reaches 4,280 m (14,042 ft), exceeding Telluride's lift top at 3,812 m (12,507 ft), creating rain-on-snow risk on the highest terrain.",
    "photo": "Telluride's upper-slope snowpack on 8 November: 115 cm (45 in) at the highest elevations, showing how depth thins rapidly below the summits."
   }
  }
